WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:06.140
In April this professional streamer had more social media interactions than LeBron James,

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:12.720
Cristiano Ronaldo and Shaquille O'Neal, making him the most popular athlete in the world.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:19.320
He also makes more than $500,000 a month jumping off a virtual bus onto an island to farm,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.360
fight loot and dance with 99 other players
in a fight to the death.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.920
And he generally wins.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.280
A lot.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.160
(Stream)

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.720
He’s played alongside the likes of Drake, Travis Scott and a number of professional athletes.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:49.500
(Stream)

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.740
He’s raised hundreds of thousands of dollars for charity.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:01:05.700
(Stream)

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.000
And even hosted his own esports event that broke the Twitch viewership record he set

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.000
when he played with the 6 God.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.200
"Welcome to Ninja Vegas 18"

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.920
"We have ourselves a fire alarm here."

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.440
"Ninja Vegas 18, I'm super excited to get started."

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.180
This is Ninja.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.920
And if you don’t recognize his face yet — it’ll only be only a matter of time

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.220
until you do.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.920
But where did this guy come from?

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.260
And how did he carry the gaming world out
of Tilted Towers?

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:51.220
(Stream)

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.720
This is his story.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:08.140
Tyler “Ninja” Blevins started his esports career in Halo, just as Halo 3 was coming out

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:13.920
and made his esports debut at MLG Orlando 2009 with team Four of a Kind.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.660
Only 19 at the time, Ninja made a name for himself as an aggressive player whose risky

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.780
playstyle quickly became his calling card.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.600
"I knew right away like this kid is going to be amazing. He's got a lot a lot of talent."

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.300
"You just basically have to run with him because he is just going to hold forward, get in people's faces."

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:42.080
"And challenge and he was a 1v1 god. He was that very individually based player."

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:48.560
But it was only when Halo Reach came out in September 2010 that Ninja truly flourished and quickly

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.920
rose to the upper echelon of Reach players.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.360
"They're kinda like Impact here, a lot of people not giving the credit they deserve."

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.700
"I couldn't agree with you more there Chris, and the thing is you have a player like Ninja on their team

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.680
"Who is going to spark. He's just got so much individual talent and Status Quo, although they have a lot of

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.500
teamwork aren't necessarily the best shots. So Ninja could really disturb this team."

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:17.040
Ninja bounced between various team between 2010-2012 but was picked up by Final Boss

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.760
at the end of 2011.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.240
In the world of Halo esports, playing for Final Boss was as big as it gets, and when

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.800
they picked up Ninja everyone knew just how good he was.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:52.100
(Casting)

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:58.580
Shortly after he joined Final Boss, the scene switched from Reach to Halo 4, it was in

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.200
that game that he finally won it all.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:20.240
(Casting)

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.840
And as he took the Halo scene by storm, Ninja
was also streaming on Twitch.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:31.800
Even at the beginning of his streaming career, when he was only broadcasting to a handful of viewers,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.320
it was clear that that he was at his best in front of an audience.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:05:00.640
(Casting)

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:06.840
After Halo 4 came Halo 2: Anniversary during which Ninja competed with Cloud9.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:13.320
He put up a string of strong finishes highlighted
by a Top 4 finish at HCS Season 1 finals.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.000
Ninja finished his Halo career during Halo 5.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:24.080
He played on Team Liquid, Renegades, Evil Geniuses and Luminosity during that era.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:31.040
His last major event as a Halo player was DreamHack Atlanta 2017 with Luminosity.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:36.020
While he was playing with Evil Geniuses he continued to play Halo, but was balancing

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.400
it with his new love - H1Z1.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.080
The zombie-style Battle Royale game helped Ninja’s stream take off in a big way.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:57.040
(Casting)

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.760
He was able to make the jump from an esports player styling in his game-of-choice, to a

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.480
full time entertainer, playing the new hot
game.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.940
"Taking a small little breaksy from Halo."

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.520
"Focusing more on other games and streaming."

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.840
Ninja’s stream began to grow larger during the summer of 2017 after the release of

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.740
Player Unknown’s Battlegrounds.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.600
PUBG made a huge splash in the streaming world.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:30.300
The Battle Royale format was perfect for crazy highlights, funny moments and an all around

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.040
viewer experience.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:41.600
(Stream)

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:47.060
Streamers flocked to the game to keep up with it’s rising popularity.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.700
The skills that Ninja picked up from his Halo days served him well, and, to an extent, transferred

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.280
to this new format.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:06.880
(Stream)

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.879
No matter what the objective of the game is, one thing started to become clear — Ninja

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.800
was lethal with a rifle.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:22.620
(Stream)

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.920
And when Fortnite: Battle Royale was released in September 2017,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:32.200
that kid who would play Halo wearing a cow on his head would never be the same.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:42.200
(Stream)

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.240
On October 3, 2017 Ninja streamed Fortnite for the first time.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:53.180
But unlike his streams now, his average viewership stayed steady at around 5,000.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:59.020
And then, as December reared its frosty head, Ninja’s stream blew up.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.620
(Stream)

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:10.540
12,000 viewers in December 2017 turned into 25,000 in January and by the time Spring came

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.880
around, he was streaming to 100,000 viewers a day.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:20.240
"It's so crazy man, times just change so much. Especially since like, I'm in uncharted territory as a streamer."

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:26.960
"I always valued, I took pride in the fact that I always read every sub and every dono."

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:31.040
"And unfortunately I had to cut out reading subs cause there's just way too many."

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:37.700
"Here we are, about a month maybe two months in where I haven't read subs and you're complimenting me

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:41.200
on reading all the donos right like, it's nuts.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.640
"I'll always do my best to make sure I never miss them."

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:50.640
A former Halo pro, turned small-time streamer was climbing to the top of the Twitch viewership rankings.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.060
And he was hauling Fortnite along with him.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:59.060
What’s unique about Ninja’s on-screen persona is that he’s able to effectively

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:04.480
combine the attitudes and styles of several Twitch personality archetypes.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.200
He can be boisterous and loud.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:24.320
(Stream)

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:28.360
He can impress you with his play skill and
in-game advice.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.160
(Stream)

00:09:32.160 --> 00:10:08.240
(Stream)

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.840
He can catch the eyes of parents with his
rants about the importance of education,

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.160
commitment to charity and focus on mental health awareness.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.660
"And honestly I encourage everyone to do that, all the kids out there you can't just drop everything and

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:27.820
focus on playing video games for a living. It's also becoming a very competitive career choice right now."

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:32.900
"You want to make sure you're securing your future, and putting in the extra time to try and make this happen

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:33.500
as well."

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.620
He has the skills needed to both capture an audience

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:41.560
(Stream)

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.180
and keep them coming back day after day.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:48.660
"And I literally remember the turning point of my entire career was when I addressed my stream

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.120
and I was just like, 'You need to understand that I love this game. But like a lot of these people who are

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.700
watching me play Fortnite, like no one is supporting the channel, no one is subscribing.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.100
I can't keep playing this game if you guys don't support me.' And that day alone I think I gained like a 1000

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:02.960
subscribers."

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:04.420
"And then after that it was over."

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:10.840
At the time Ninja’s stream started to grow, Battle Royale games were still relatively in their infancy.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:17.020
And in a lot of ways, Fortnite’s success has gone hand-in-hand with Ninja’s growth as a streamer.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.920
"It's been interesting to see the way that he's grown.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:26.280
And Fortnite has been, very successful for him. People enjoy watching him because he is,

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:31.740
Terrifyingly good. Something I (don't tell him) a little jealous."

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:37.160
"Sometimes I lookat it and I think, man did EPIC just make this game specifically for him?"

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:45.480
"His personality on stream and the way that the game is played at a higher entertainment style."

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.560
"Those two things they go hand in hand."

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:52.440
As he got better, more people watched him.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:55.380
And more people played Fortnite.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.820
And when he became one of the best players in the world, Fortnite lept into the mainstream

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.260
in a big way.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:08.520
"Not long ago everyone was freaking out that the big video game publishers were in the crosshairs

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:13.700
of these popular new Battle Royale style multiplayer games, there's Fortnite."

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:18.000
"This is a new game called Fortnite. It's drawing millions of players."

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:24.260
"Let's now talk Fortnite shall we? It's the newest video game craze spreading across fast among kids

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:25.860
college kids, even celebrities.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:31.420
And we’ve seen him capitalize on that by expanding his reach within the Fortnite ecosystem.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:37.230
Ninja’s brought streaming personalities like Myth, DrLupo and TimTheTatMan into his

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:41.170
bubble of popularity and helped them grow alongside him.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:46.200
Just as he drops them a shield potion in-game, he’ll also plug their stream.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:52.220
With his growing popularity, his posse, his famous win-streak headband and that catchy

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:53.600
pon pon dance,

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:58.760
(Stream)

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:01.680
Ninja’s built a stream that feels close to a sitcom.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.320
(Laughtrack)

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:09.000
And Ninja is the fearless protagonist — saving his teammates at Tilted Towers and cracking

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:13.900
jokes amidst the familiar backdrop of his basement guest bedroom.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:19.551
February saw Ninja average more than 46 thousand viewers at a time, making him one of the top

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.380
streamers in the world.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:27.300
He’d climbed the ladder to the pinnacle of the gaming world — the top of the Twitch rankings.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:32.680
But on March 14th, 2018 Ninja proved that he wasn’t going to settle for being Twitch’s

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.120
flavour of the month.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:37.860
It was time to break out of the gaming echo chamber.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:44.300
Ninja played squads with rappers Drake and Travis Scott as well as Pittsburgh Steelers

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:47.340
wide receiver JuJu Smith-Schuster.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:51.120
And, well, it kind of broke the internet.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:14:04.060
(Stream)

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:11.300
The stream broke the Twitch individual streaming record with around 630,000 concurrent viewers.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:14.840
In just one night Ninja became a household name.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.980
And his viewership ballooned.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:23.220
During March, Ninja streamed to over 90,000 viewers every day.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.320
He had Drake on for a second stream.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:56.100
(Stream)

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:03.060
And all of a  sudden every Twitch viewer, consumer of pop culture and their extended family knew who

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:04.620
Ninja was.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:09.220
So what’s different about a Ninja stream now that he’s sitting in his basement in

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.720
front of 100,000 viewers all the time?

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.760
Ninja sees the relationship between viewer and streamer unlike any other.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.980
"Absolutely like I did a recent interview and that was one of their questions was like

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:26.980
Would I consider myself like a personality or a character.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.580
And absolutely not."

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.840
"I'm the exact same person "

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:34.240
"I have never seen a harder worker, in my mind it goes back to the guy that was like I have to be up for these

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.420
600 subs."

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:38.180
"And he's the same person. It's just paid off."

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:41.200
He provides a social contract.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:47.680
He’s willing to communicate honestly, in a way that feels sincere in exchange for viewership

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.120
and subscription.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:54.120
He makes you feel like an integral part of the community he’s building, and for that

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.260
your money feels well spent.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:58.880
Ninja’s the good guy.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:03.640
The guy you’d want to show to your parents to defend the time you spend playing Fortnite.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:09.320
"Believe in yourself, and work hard. Know what you're worth. Know what you're capable of."

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.640
"Resilience, is very important."

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:16.880
But someone you can also kick back and watch
with your friends.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:29.280
(Stream)

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:35.200
All of this combined with his consistent streaming schedule and clear focus on having a unified

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:37.880
brand, has lead to his success.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:42.760
Oh yeah, did we mention that he’s one of the best Fortnite players in the world?

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:54.280
(Stream)

00:16:54.280 --> 00:17:12.000
(Stream)

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:17.760
At the end of April, Ninja continued to add to his storied few months with Ninja Vegas,

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:22.860
his own $50,000 Fortnite tournament at the Esports Arena in Las Vegas.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:28.300
The event, pitted Ninja against some of the game’s best, where players received $2,500

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:32.440
for killing Ninja and $2,500 for a Victory Royale.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:55.040
(Casting)

00:17:55.040 --> 00:18:02.640
This continued in ten flights over two days for a total of 50k in prizes.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:08.400
And any time Ninja won, $2,500 would be donated to the Alzheimer’s Association.

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:20.640
(Casting)

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:25.240
To say that it was a massive success would be an understatement.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:30.440
Not only was it the first major competitive Fortnite event but at one point it had more

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:37.080
than 667,000 concurrent viewers, surpassing the famous Drizzy stream.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.840
Charitable endeavours like this have become a staple of Ninja’s rise to the top.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.940
He seems to be doing a new charity stream or initiative every other week.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:55.500
"$74655 in nine hours, that's about 10k an hour 8k an hour. That's incredible man you guys are amazing."

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:01.480
In the past he’s spent time raising money to support suicide awareness and the Alzheimer’s Association.

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:08.300
"What we have done and accomplished today, is remarkable."

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:16.920
"I mean, there's nothing more to say. In under eight hours we have raised $113,000."

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:21.620
"A lot of the proceeds tonight went to charity right, and it was for the Alzheimer’s Association."

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:27.520
"All the dollars from the ticket sales will be matched by ID tech and we'll be making a contribution as well."

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.540
"Thank you so much."

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.440
More recently he sold branded t-shirts in collaboration with streamer and duos partner

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:35.600
DrLupo.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:41.600
Combined they set out to raise $500,000 for St. Judes research hospital.

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:44.540
So here we are, in 2018.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:49.380
And so far this month an ex Halo pro turned Battle Royale streamer is streaming to an

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:53.980
average of over 125,000 people every day.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.480
And he isn’t slowing down.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:09.160
(Stream)

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:14.480
In March, 2018 he was ranked third in the world in social interactions right behind

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:17.520
Christiano Renaldo and Lebron James.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:22.780
This was the first time a pro gamer cracked the Top 10 of this list.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:28.720
In April, he was the #1 ranked athlete in the world on the same chart.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:35.980
As of mid-May he has almost 7 million Twitch followers, more than 10 million Youtube Subscribers

00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:39.020
and over 2 million Twitter followers.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:44.160
It feels like he’s streaming with or making appearances with the most famous people in

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.700
the world on a weekly basis.

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:49.960
He’s a household name in the truest sense.

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:55.620
The amalgamation of every gaming-meets-mainstream storyline combined.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:21:01.920
Despite its stranglehold on the gaming world, it’s still unclear just how far Fortnite will go.

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:07.180
The competitive landscape is mostly made-up of third party and community events.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:13.500
One of these events — May’s Keemstar Tournament
— proved just how lethal he could be

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.440
in a competitive environment.

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:32.720
(Stream)

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:39.780
Just days after that event, Epic Games announced a jaw dropping $100,000,000 commitment

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:44.160
to competitive Fortnite events during the 2018-2019 season.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.880
Meaning that Fortnite esports, is likely to take off in a big way.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:52.020
(Stream)

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:58.140
"I think there's like a 100,000,000 in prize pool now. Are you going to try and make some money off of it."

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:03.460
"I feel like I would have better luck doing just about anything than making money off of Fortnite."

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.800
"So you don't think it's a viable option for people unless you're the best gamer in the world."

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.640
"I feel if you're like Ninja or something then totally."

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.560
"That's true, Ninja would be pretty good at it."

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:19.260
On Tuesday May 1st, he streamed to 190,000 people just after the newest Fortnite season started.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:26.840
Almost 200 thousand people watched Ninja play Fortnite on a regular boring Tuesday afternoon.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:30.480
It’s all happening right in front of our eyes.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:37.100
The craziest part about it is that it’s still growing with no signs of slowing down.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:46.280
(Pon Pon)

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.800
(Casting)

00:22:50.000 --> 00:23:24.560
(Music)

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:30.280
"I think that that literal one match with Drake, made it okay for everyone to play video games."

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:47.280
